preConditionHook:
// THE 3/10 OSCILLATOR -- Grimes's fast line, in the settled form (merge
// decision 2): the difference of two SIMPLE averages of the close,
//     osc = average of close over fastLen bars, less the average
//           of close over slowLen bars
// with fastLen 3 and slowLen 10 as the book's defaults. Never the MACD word.
// EL's Average RECOMPUTES from scratch every bar (VERIFIED, 2,808/2,808 bars;
// it is NOT a rolling accumulator like the WFSafe_ wrappers) and Summation
// adds the newest bar first with a single divide at the end -- which is how
// the loops below run. Both averages run over a PRICE expression, which has
// real bar history, so they are recomputed from the bars rather than mirrored;
// no history of osc itself is read here, so there is no ring buffer and no
// warm-up guard.
//
// THE EXIT: the fast line has CHANGED SIGN at least once since the trade was
// entered -- the divergence that justified the trade has reset, so close the
// position. The sign is captured ONCE, on the fill bar, and latched; it is not
// re-read afterwards.
//
// THE LATCH, not a variable-lag read. The mined candidate spelled this
// `oscFast[0] * oscFast[BarsSinceEntry] <= 0`, a read of the series at a
// VARIABLE offset; capturing the sign on the fill bar is the corpus's
// convention for exactly this (StopAtSignalBarRangeMultiple). BarsSinceEntry
// is VERIFIED to be 0 on the bar the entry fills, to restart through a
// reversal (so a reversal re-latches without ever passing through flat), and
// to read 0 WHILE FLAT -- which is why the latch is armed only when
// MarketPosition is non-zero, and disarmed when it returns to zero.
//
// Not MovingAverageCross at 3/10, which merge decision 2 settles IS the zero
// cross of this line: that is an ENTRY, it fires only on the crossing bar, and
// it goes through EL's `crosses` STATE MACHINE. This is an EXIT that stays
// TRUE for the rest of the position once the sign has flipped, and an Entry
// rule cannot sit in the exit pane -- the ConsecutiveCloseExit /
// MomentumConsecutiveBars precedent.
//
// SIDES IDENTICAL: the test is "the sign is not the one it was at entry",
// which is already relative to the trade; the generator's own MarketPosition
// guard on the exit applies the long side to a long and the short side to a
// short.
//
// osc is a difference of averages of price, so the sign test is a test on a
// DIFFERENCE and the rule passes the shift test. A bar sitting exactly on zero
// fires neither branch: each branch requires the value to be STRICTLY on the
// other side.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: max(fastLen, slowLen) - 1.
oscFast = 0.0;
for (int i = 0; i < (int)fastLen; ++i) {
    oscFast += close[i];
}
oscFast = oscFast / (int)fastLen;
oscSlow = 0.0;
for (int i = 0; i < (int)slowLen; ++i) {
    oscSlow += close[i];
}
oscSlow = oscSlow / (int)slowLen;
osc = oscFast - oscSlow;
if (ctx.MarketPosition() == 0) {
    armed = false;
}
if (ctx.MarketPosition() != 0 && ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0) {
    armed = true;
    if (el_gt(osc, 0.0)) {
        entryUp = true;
    } else {
        entryUp = false;
    }
}
